'''''Okheng''''' (沃興) was a polity on the northwestern coast of [[Lurandia]], at the bay of [[Oktalwa]], existing from 1521 to 1535. It was formed from the territory of the [[Akwesa]] chiefdom and the commercial establishment of the merchant house of the [[Sūn-heng Hâng]], and was governed as a joint holding of the merchant family and the chiefly family. It was administered in practice by the company.

The name is a Zong coinage, formed from the first element of Oktalwa and the second of Sūn-heng, and it was by that name that the polity was known to the colonial administration. No other name for it is recorded.

In 1535 the territory was conveyed to the [[Zong colonial administration]] for 4,000 taels of gold, and the polity ceased to exist.

== Origins ==

In 1511 the Akwesa ruler [[Hokta]] granted a plot of land at the bay to a party of Zong traders in exchange for ten pigs. The grant was recorded by the Zong as a purchase, and Akwesa practice did not treat land as property that could be alienated. The [[Chiu Bûn-hái|Chiu]] family's merchant house, trading as the Sūn-heng Hâng, built a depot on the plot and operated it as a manned outpost from that year.

The company assisted the Akwesa in their wars against neighbouring [[Kawesa]] chiefdoms, and the territory under Akwesa control expanded in consequence. The arrangement was a straightforward exchange of the resources each party possessed: the company supplied men, shipping and money; the chiefdom supplied land, legitimacy and access.

In 1521 the alliance was sealed by the marriage of Hokta's daughter [[Chinowa]] to [[Chiu Khéng-gio̍k]], the son of the company's founder. From that year the territory was governed jointly by the two families, and it is from this point that Okheng is usually dated.

== Government ==

The polity was administered by the company. The Sūn-heng Hâng held the commercial establishment, the ships and the armed men, and it conducted the diplomacy and the wars through which the territory had been enlarged. The chiefly family supplied the standing that made the arrangement possible in Akwesa terms: land held by grant, the allegiance of the people living on it, and a claim of rule that the company could not have asserted on its own account.

Okheng possessed no institutions of its own that the colonial record describes. No assembly, court, treasury or body of officials is attested; there is no evidence of a coinage, a written law, or a system of taxation distinct from the arrangement between the two families and their dependants. The territory was run as a commercial enterprise with a ruling family attached to it, and the colonial administration's objection to it was framed in precisely those terms: that a private merchant was conducting diplomacy, waging war and expanding a territory in his own name.

The company remitted no revenue to the Zong colonial administration. No agreement between Okheng and the colony existed at any point in the polity's history, and the administration took no action against it between 1522, when word of the arrangement reached [[Anping]], and 1535, when it resolved to acquire the site.

== Territory and population ==

The territory comprised the bay of Oktalwa and its surrounding country, enlarged by the campaigns conducted with the company's support. The portion held by the Zong at the time of the purchase has been measured at approximately 3,300 km², a coastal tract of some 97 by 55 kilometres; this figure is a modern estimate from the surviving descriptions and is not exact.

The population was overwhelmingly Akwesa, with a small Zong presence consisting of the company's employees, its garrison and a handful of merchant families. The Zong settlement is unlikely to have exceeded a few hundred persons at any point, against an Akwesa population estimated at some 12,000 before the epidemics of the 1520s.

That population was much reduced by the time Okheng ended. Plague, carried to the bay by rats aboard the trading vessels, killed Hokta in 1527 and recurred in the years that followed; the Akwesa are recorded as having suffered heavy losses, and the chiefdom was described as much reduced by the deaths. The polity that was sold in 1535 was accordingly far smaller than the one that had been formed in 1521, and its ruler governed a people that the trade had already diminished.

== End of the polity ==

In 1535 the colonial administration determined to acquire the site as the harbour for its northern presence. [[Chiu Khéng-gio̍k]], who had ruled Okheng since 1527, was paid 4,000 taels of gold and was at the same time threatened with invasion and with execution for treason should he refuse. No charge was laid.

The sum was assessed as the value of the works the company had built, and no allowance was made for the land, the political entity or the trade. The transaction was recorded as a purchase. The territory became a sub-territory of the Zong colony under Chiu Khéng-gio̍k as its provisional administrator, and the polity of Okheng ceased to exist.

== Aftermath ==

The name Okheng did not survive the polity. The territory was administered as part of the Zong colony until 1544, when the office of provisional administrator was dissolved and the colonial territory was divided along the [[Qingjiang]] river into the [[Sinchew Colony]] and the [[Louyang Colony]]. Chiu Khéng-gio̍k was removed in the same period for his part in the [[Tamsui Plot]], and the former ruler of the Akwesa thereafter served the colony as its ambassador to his own former people.

In 1538, three years after the purchase, the colonial administration advanced the claim that Hokta had been King of the Northern Mahiks, on the ground that he had granted his land to the company and the company had conveyed it. The claim supplied a ground of jurisdiction over the lands of the six chiefdoms that had never granted the colony anything, and it fixed the boundary between the Zong territory and that of the [[Hachinawa|Hahkina]].

The Akwesa survived the [[First Mahik War]] of 1538 to 1542 intact, being a client of the colony rather than a target of it, and continued thereafter as a minority community within the Sinchew colony. The modern collective name for the peoples of the region, [[Kawesa]], descends from the name of the chiefdom on which Okheng had been built.

